heinlein@matt.ksu.ksu.edu (Robert A. Heinlein) writes:

> So Mike the foretold Barbarian rushes up in the "y" direction to get near
> a Yeeghnho(sp?) that i hadn't tamed yet... in the process the said Demon Lord
> got in the way... i was just rushing in that direction and was still
> hitting "y" when it said REALLY attack the Yeeghnho? (y/n)? 

I've never fallen prey to this one myself, since I use the numberpad option
for moving around, but I've always thought that was dangerous.  Maybe
the game could require a capital Y/N response to a "really attack"
confirm if you're not using the numberpad.

Better still, let's make the user interface a little smarter.  The act
of trying to walk is very different from the act of attempting to
slay a monster, from the perspective of my character.  How could a
competent character be so stupid as to attack "by mistake" when just
trying to walk around?

My solution to this is to make attacking sort of like picking up
objects.  Just as '@' toggles autopickup mode, the 'F' key (or
whatever) could toggle move/fight mode.  Trying to move into a monster
should be a no-op, like walking over an item when pickup mode is
off.  It shouldn't even cost you a turn.  Then if you turn fight mode
on, attacking a blank square is a wild swing.


SUMMARY OF MY PROPOSED CHANGES TO USER INTERFACE

#swim	enter water.  prompts for a direction.

<dir>	in Fight mode: attack in <dir> (wild swing if nothing there)
	in Move mode:  move in <dir> (will not attack or enter water)

F	toggle between fight-mode and move-mode.  Shows mode.

m<dir>	move in <dir> (as above), regardless of mode

f<dir>	attack in <dir> (as above) regardless of mode


The actual keys I selected might not be ideal, but you get the idea.
This way, the player at the keyboard is unlikely to make a typographical
mistake, and accidentally instruct his character to do something that
just isn't done "by mistake".  The changes are minimal (it would seem)
and I think it would make the game a little more playable, without
making it any more winnable :-)

